{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"J6 HELD-OUT VALIDATION — the oracle found a REAL parity gap the happy-path spec could not.\nTIERING: standard/mid (sonnet), named on dispatch. Result shape: 664 LOC across 4 files, all far under the 800 cap (transport 297, backend 273, settings 77, __init__ 17) — the implementer checked the cap itself this time, after it bit twice earlier in this epic.\nFIRST VALIDATION: 10 of 14 passed. Four failures, and I investigated each rather than assuming implementer fault:\n * THREE were MY error. My held-out helper called build_client_from_settings(url=..., token=..., ca_bundle=...) — kwargs I invented. The real seam takes a JiraDataCenterSettings object (settings.py:40, a NamedTuple). I had over-specified an internal signature instead of driving the production seam. Fixed my test to construct a REAL settings object and swap only the library class, so the assertions now see exactly what a live run passes to jira.JIRA.\n * ONE WAS A REAL GAP, and worth the whole exercise. transport.py's docstring claims its retry policy 'mirrors acli_rest._rest_urlopen_with_retry ... exactly'. It retried requests' ConnectionError/Timeout only. But acli_rest explicitly ALSO retries BUILTIN TimeoutError, and its docstring records why: 'read-timeout from ssl/socket layer'. Since Python 3.10 socket.timeout IS builtin TimeoutError, so a raw ssl/socket read-timeout escapes requests' wrappers — the exact fault the Cloud path already survives and the DC path would not have. Verified the claim by reading acli_rest rather than trusting either side. Added TimeoutError to the retryable tuple with the acli_rest citation in a comment.\nMUTATION / TEETH — and a process catch:\n * MUT: disable TLS on the DEFAULT (no-ca_bundle) path => test_tls_verification_is_never_disabled RED. This is the assertion the plan review saved: phrased as 'no verify=False kwarg' it would have been VACUOUS, since pycontribs has no such kwarg and reads verify from its options dict.\n * MUT: set options['verify']=False on the ca_bundle path => the ca_bundle test RED.\n * MUT: drop the self-registration import from adapters/__init__.py => test_the_dc_backend_is_registered_and_selectable RED, naming the registered keys. That is blocker B1's exact runtime failure, now caught in CI instead of at an operator's first run.\n * MUT: retry everything (including HTTP errors) => test_an_http_error_is_attempted_exactly_once RED — and it took 7.37s, the real 2s+5s backoff actually running, which is independent proof the mutation applied.\n * PROCESS CATCH: my FIRST attempt at that last mutation edited 'return (_req_exc.ConnectionError, ...)' — UNREACHABLE here, because requests is not installed in this venv so the code takes the ImportError branch. It 'passed' 9 tests and proved nothing. Same silent-no-op trap as earlier in this epic; caught it by noticing the run was 0.47s (no backoff) and redid it against the reachable branch.\nPRE-FLIGHT: make lint GREEN (after I fixed 4 issues in MY OWN test files, including ruff B017 'do not assert blind Exception' — replaced pytest.raises(Exception) with a named FakeHttpError, which is a stronger assertion anyway). make typecheck GREEN (395 files). ACLI CONFIRMED UNTOUCHED by git diff. 'import rebar' works with no jira package installed (the lazy-import contract). tests/external/live_jira_dc/ skips cleanly: 9 skipped, no errors. Full suite running."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785430477248049001,"uuid":"5208565a-1047-4950-8f64-f0fc9f38acf7"}